THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
SRC_001 replaces the skeptical fixed-point with a proof-theoretic grounded consequence relation whose extension is the set of formulas derivable in a monotone sequent calculus, claiming reinstatement is a derived theorem. However, reintatement as a *derived property* requires proving that whenever a former attacker is refuted, the previously suspended target becomes derivable without an explicit postulate. In a monotone Horn-fragment least Herbrand model, derivability is purely additive and never retracts, so the calculus has no native mechanism to show that the discharge of an attack *causes* a previously underivable node to become derivable. The target merely labels the attacker rule as 'discharges an attack when the attacker is refuted', but in a Horn setting this discharge is not a negation-as-failure or deletion operation; it is just another derivation step. Thus the target asserts reinstatement is derived but does not exhibit the derivation rule combination that actually yields it, leaving the guarantee unmet.

TARGET:
Replace the skeptical fixed-point over labels with a proof-theoretic grounded consequence relation: a node is accepted iff there exists a finite derivation tree using two rules — an attacker rule that discharges an attack when the attacker is refuted, and a warrant rule that introduces a committed root — and the extension is the set of formulas derivable in this monotone sequent calculus. The fixed point is recovered as the least Herbrand model of the derivation rules, preserving determinism and polynomial cost because derivability in a Horn fragment is P-complete; reinstatement is a derived theorem, not an extra postulate. This makes provenance internal to the derivation rather than an attached trace. Criticism: it may make the semantics less modular and harder to extend with quantitative information.